.TH BOOTSTASH 8 "September 2026" "bootstash" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bootstash \- operator tools for bootstashd
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bootstash
\fBcheck-config\fR
[\fB\-defaults\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-secrets\fR \fIpath\fR]
.PP
.B bootstash
\fBprovision-google\fR
[\fIoptions\fR]
.PP
.B bootstash
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bootstash
is the operator CLI for the bootstash daemon. It does not serve HTTP or
call PAM.
.B provision-google
writes Google OIDC client keys into
.IR /etc/bootstash/oidc-google.json ;
it does not create the Google OAuth web client and it does not edit
.IR /etc/default/bootstash .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check-config
Parse dist defaults
.RI ( /usr/lib/bootstash/default-dist ),
.IR /etc/default/bootstash ,
.IR /etc/bootstash/oidc-google.json ,
every
.B BIND
spec, and TLS files when set. Exit 0 if the daemon could start (missing
interfaces are not an error here). Same as
.B bootstashd \-t .
.TP
.B provision-google
Print a four-step Google console recipe with a recommended
value at each step, then install the Web application
client JSON the console downloads as
.IR /etc/bootstash/oidc-google.json
.RB ( \-json
or a prompted path).
Reads
.B PUBLIC_URL
from
.I /etc/default/bootstash
(or
.BR \-origin ).
If unset, the same
.B hostname \-f
plus listen-port default as
.BR bootstashd
is used.
Needs root (or
.BR sudo )
to write that file; it does not write TLS certs (the Let’s Encrypt
hook does).
It does not create the Google OAuth web client.
.TP
.B version
Print the version from
.B git describe
(\fB\-\-version\fR,
.BR \-version ,
.B \-V
are aliases).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-config " path"
Operator config to read. Default:
.IR /etc/default/bootstash .
.TP
.BI \-secrets " path"
OIDC client secrets file. Default:
.IR /etc/bootstash/oidc-google.json .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bootstashd (8),
.BR bootstash (5),
.IR /usr/share/doc/bootstash/QUICKSTART.md
